      "content": "I see what you did there. It's still feeling very \"keyword heavy\"—I'm noticing that almost every single sentence is packed with two or three of those bold terms. I'm worried that if I submit this, the human reviewer will think I'm being pretentious or, worse, that I'm hiding a lack of actual ideas behind big words.\n\nI need to move away from the \"list\" feel. Can we try a different approach? Instead of trying to hit the concepts through abstract nouns like \"ontological re-evaluation\" or \"heuristic pathways,\" can you write a paragraph that focuses on a specific, concrete example? \n\nLike, talk about how a specific piece of tech (maybe broadband access or a local community app) affects a real interaction in a rural town. Try to explain the complexity of that interaction without relying so heavily on the jargon list. I'm not saying avoid the big concepts entirely, but let's see if we can actually *describe* the phenomena instead of just naming them.",
